  </Chapter>

  <!-- 07 Reflection -->
  <Chapter id="reflection" num={num('reflection')} label="Reflection">
    <h2 class="c-h2" data-split-lines>Navigation is never <span class="serif">done</span></h2>
    <p class="lead" data-reveal="fade-up">
      The new sidebar launched in September 2026, and leadership was happy with the result. I don’t have access to post-launch analytics, so the numbers here come from the internal test — not production.
    </p>
    <div class="prose" data-reveal="fade-up">
      <p>
        The project taught me that navigation keeps absorbing new features. A clean first version is only the start: if every team adds its own categories again, the original problem comes back. If I continue this work, I’d propose turning a few questions into shared rules:
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="nlist" data-reveal="fade-up">
      <li><span>01</span><span>When does a feature deserve a new <strong>top-level entry</strong>?</span></li>
      <li><span>02</span><span>Does the name use <strong>words merchants actually use</strong>?</span></li>
      <li><span>03</span><span>Can it fit an <strong>existing, sensible task group</strong> instead?</span></li>
      <li><span>04</span><span>Who reviews <strong>cross-module navigation changes</strong> together?</span></li>
      <li><span>05</span><span>How do we keep measuring <strong>wrong turns, abandonment and completion</strong> with real merchants?</span></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="fineprint" data-reveal="fade-up">These are recommendations, not an established process.</p>

    <blockquote class="testimonial" data-reveal="fade-up">
      <p class="pull">My biggest contribution: turning a vague complaint into <em>a product problem we could test</em>.</p>
      <p class="prose">
        Understand the whole system, propose a structure, align with every feature team, validate it with a comparative test — and ship within the timeline, with clear owners for what comes next.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </Chapter>

